.TH PCBROUTE 1
.SH NAME
pcbroute \- route the copper of a board
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pcbroute
[
.B \-v
] [
.B \-g
.I group
] [
.B \-L
.I lib
] ...
.I board.pcb
[
.I lock
]
.RI > new.lock
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Pcbroute
reads the board source,
.IR pcb (5),
and a lock with the parts placed, and writes the lock with the copper.
What is kept, by connection, is kept as it is; the rest is routed anew
among the copper kept, which nothing moves.
Routing is best effort
.RI ( pcb (5)):
the same source and lock need not give the same copper, and later
routers may be stochastic.
.PP
Each pad of a net of a plane layer gets a via on a neck track, the
nearest place clear of all else, its ring the via gap from the pads of
its net and the mask web from every opening of the mask, away from its
part or the way a
.B via
statement says; a pad of a pin with a plated hole needs none.
A small part of two pins (each pad 1 mm2 or less, an 0402 or 0603)
must heat alike at its pads: its pad on a plane joins by a neck no wider
than a track, whatever the net's width, and its pad on the plane's own
layer is relieved, cleared from the pour and joined to it by a neck out
along the pad's longer side; any other pad on its plane's layer is in
the pour.
A net with pins on parts not placed yet (of an
.B unplaced
statement) waits whole: none of it is routed, and its copper leaves the
lock; a plane net still gets its vias.
Every other net is routed on the top layer as a tree of connections,
each from a pad's center to another's (of a long pad, twice as long as
wide or more, from any point of the line between its ends, each a half
width in from the pad's end): the pins of its
.B fork
statements first (the two last pins joined, then the first pin routed
from the middle of that copper, by its length, on a grid laid through
that point, to its pad), then the pins of its
.B path
statements, then the pins of its
.B width
statements, then the nearest pad not yet joined to the nearest joined,
by the cheapest path over a grid of 0.05 mm in eight ways laid through
the first pad's center, keeping the clearance from all copper of other
nets (a run between two cells as well as the cells, and the lock's
rounding) and the edge rule from the outline and the cutouts, their round
corners round.
The search keeps to one window of 256 by 256 cells (12.8 mm), laid
about the middle of what it joins, allocated once and never grown, the
obstacles marked only inside it; so a board of any size routes in a
few megabytes.
A connection whose ends lie more than 7.4 mm apart along x or y (what
fits the window with room round its ends and for the slide) is routed
in sections along the straight line between its pads, as few as keep
each that short, each in the window laid about it, each joint a point of the line
on the grid, or slid across the line by up to 1.5 mm off copper and out
of cutouts; the sections make one track.
A track leaves and enters a pad along its longer side (any way for a
round or square pad) and turns only outside it; pads in line are joined
by one straight track through their centers.
A long pad is left or entered any of the four ways, at whichever point
of its line makes the cheapest path: a pad beside it is joined straight
into its side, square to the line, and one past its end along the line;
a point where the track would break the edge rule is never taken.
Each connection takes its own width, of a
.B width
.I PIN PIN W
statement naming its pins, else the net's.
The copper is kept by connection: one keeps its copper if its net's
statements, its pads and its width are as they were and its copper is
clear of the pads as they stand now and keeps the edge rule, so an edit
makes anew only the connections it touches.
A net that finds no way is put first and all are routed again.
With
.B \-v
each net and via is told on stderr; a part is named by its name and
designator, there and in the faults.
The lock, with the parts placed, comes from the file
.IR lock ,
or stdin.
A run routes each group of a
.B block
statement alone, the deepest first, each in its section of the lock,
then the board, the blocks' copper taken from their sections, but for a
connection that breaks the edge rule on the board (a group alone has no
outline), which is made anew.
A copy
.RB ( block
.I COPY
.B as
.IR SOURCE )
is not routed: after SOURCE, its sections become SOURCE's, renamed part
for part and net for net.
With
.B \-g
.I group
it routes that group alone, as pcbplace laid it out: the nets whose pads
are all its own and the vias of its pads, with no outline; the copper of
a block in it is its lock's, moved with its parts and kept.
.SH SEE ALSO
pcb(5), pcbplace(1), pcbcheck(1), pcbgerber(1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
FILE: net NAME: and what failed: no way from PAD to PAD, stuck at X Y
(the point of the search nearest the target, or the joint that found no
clear place), or no room for the via of PAD, with what is in the way;
exit status 1.
